WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Parsing and trusting are different decisions.

2
00:00:02.136 --> 00:00:11.035
A parser may successfully extract a header, version, length, and body, but that does not prove who sent the message, whether it was changed in

3
00:00:11.035 --> 00:00:17.798
transit, whether it belongs to the current session, or whether it is safe to process in the current state.

4
00:00:17.798 --> 00:00:24.561
When binary messages cross trust boundaries or affect sensitive state, the design may need transport security, message authentication, signatures,

5
00:00:24.561 --> 00:00:28.120
sequence numbers, timestamps, or freshness checks according to system requirements.

6
00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:35.240
The right control depends on the environment and threat model, but the implementation should use approved cryptographic libraries, TLS where

7
00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:38.443
appropriate, or platform mechanisms rather than custom cryptographic logic.

8
00:00:38.443 --> 00:00:42.359
Integrity checks should happen before sensitive use when policy requires them.

9
00:00:42.359 --> 00:00:50.546
That does not always mean before every byte is parsed, because the parser may need a bounded envelope to locate the integrity data.

10
00:00:50.546 --> 00:00:58.733
It does mean that the application should not act on fields, allocate large resources based on trusted meaning, or change sensitive state until

11
00:00:58.733 --> 00:01:01.224
required authenticity and integrity conditions are satisfied.

12
00:01:01.224 --> 00:01:07.631
Repeated valid messages can still be unsafe if the protocol does not define freshness, idempotency, or sequence behavior.

13
00:01:07.631 --> 00:01:15.818
A payment instruction, state transition, or command may need a sequence number, nonce, timestamp window, or server-side record showing it has already

14
00:01:15.818 --> 00:01:16.530
been handled.

15
00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:21.158
Those rules should be part of the protocol contract, not a side note.

16
00:01:21.158 --> 00:01:25.429
A defensive design keeps authentication, integrity, and replay controls visible in review.

17
00:01:25.429 --> 00:01:33.616
The parser extracts structure, the validation layer checks policy, and the application acts only after the message is both well-formed and trusted

18
00:01:33.616 --> 00:01:35.040
for the current context.
